Mr moodier knows the professional services agreement is approved next item is number 24A on page five report detailing planning and development department findings in accordance with city council resolution number 6489 regarding recommended amendments to the UDC that better balance the city's long-term tree canopy and environmental goals with the practical needs of smaller lot development and housing affordability considerations mr bloom you have an update for us mayor members of council Charles Bloom plan development director um first of all what we will be asking today is that you all acknowledge receipt of this this is similar to other reports like ones for the urban renewal authority that we have given before so this will not be referred to committee uh

this is in followup to the resolution that was passed or adopted back in October which asked the planning and development department to to provide a report within 45 days regarding recommendation recommended amendments to the UDC that would better balance small lot development and housing affordability considerations one thing I would like to note this isn't a discussion point regarding should trees be required with neighborhoods should street trees be required should ornamental trees be able to substitute it really was geared at looking at how does the street tree requirement for larger canopy trees um relate to smaller lots that have utilities in close proximity and how does that affect overall affordability um 45 days is a a pretty tight timeline but we were able to do it um

we ended up having a lot of discussion within staff uh prior to inviting the stakeholders to try to figure out the best way to tackle this and dissect exactly what was expected as part of this resolution um in the end we had a meeting on October um 6 with staff where we had finalized our direction as to how we're going to move forward with this item shortly after that meeting uh we had invited a total of uh it was about 14 stakeholders um that were from all across the industry in construction to attend a meeting that next week um that was sent out by calendar invite 7 days prior to the meeting uh some of those folks I don't think they were aware of it initially and

we called to follow up with them to alert them of that um in the memorandum we have folks that were represented at the meeting uh we had a local realtor uh two folks uh very familiar with construction of housing on small lots we had several landscape architects we had a representative from the state forester department rooted in Cheyenne uh several landscape uh businesses here including Big Bird Landscaping and Magnum Tree Service we had the city forester uh we had um other staff representatives and a representative from the board of public utilities um during the invitation process there was an invite to Black Hills Energy and we had a lot of discussion with them but over time we've worked really well with Black Hills Energy especially when it comes to their overhead utilities that um

are more oftentimes a limit on these sites um in those cases uh we have a process built into our development regulations that allows for uh smaller ornamental trees to be placed so they don't interfere with those overhead utilities um in the end um it was discussed and and noted in the discussion that occurred after about a 30-minute presentation on the trees um that there are challenges that are oftentimes unique when placing large canopy trees or any tree or landscaping within a small front yard area uh it gets more challenging when a sidewalk waiver is granted however the developers did clearly state they do like that sidewalk waiver they do like the attached sidewalk because it holds up better uh

during construction but that's not what our code says right now you have to have special permission for that but they said those challenges are things like utilities uh there are challenges um that um are are uh there uh with water and sewer but it's not impossible to plant those trees in those locations one of the primary issues that they had indicated is that when they called the 811 service for locates when they're putting the utilities in not all the time the franchise utilities were properly located causing unnecessary cuts um but they said with further coordination um that could be something that could be alleviated um secondly there was a discussion about how that message was relayed to the developers and

the builders as to when that tree needs to be put in specifically in most Novembers the weather would be a lot different outside and folks would not be aware of well do we have to put the street tree in today or we don't get a a CEO that's not the case we need to do a better job of getting our information out to say you can provide a financial security where that tree can be delayed and have a planting for 6 months or so until the weather is nice um in addition we uh did look at the overall cost of housing uh from permit based on permit valuations that were provided as well as uh

average lot cost in the community and the average cost of a tree um that overall cost for a tree even rounding up to $1,000 for install um it equated to approximately one-third of 1% of the total construction cost of a 1,600 square ft finished floor area home those are ones that are commonly being built um up on Thomas and Ridge right now in the Saddle Ridge development and there are some other ones in Whitney Ranch that are floating right around that square footage i in the end the consensus of those that were present um toward at the end of the meeting there was that some of the key takeaways with with our street trees are number one uh

they really are an aesthetic item trying to build that broader canopy number two winter maintenance continues to be the biggest issue we have winter watering specifically where people don't water their trees during the winter trees need water during the winter if you have new trees right now they probably need water so you should water them um in addition there needed to be a lot more descriptions and access to our recommended tree planting list for street trees um as of publication of this memorandum we've ensured that the street tree list is now online it's on our website under our supplemental information and we're going to continue to expand that not only just to have the list because everyone's not going to know what the list is but essentially create a a book that has images and

pictures so you know what you're getting and then identifying which ones work in tight areas and smaller areas and have lesser uh of a of a span and coverage um over them um secondly we understand that there's additional need for better um education regarding winter watering um so we can look at ways to provide information to folks as they complete the development process and the final thing was that we needed to look to continue to improve our interdep departmental communication regarding when plans shall be submitted for tree plantings um and in fact with a subdivision and road development one of the requirements under present code right now is that a uh tree street tree planting plan be provided um

that has generally happened but we need to make sure that that plan uh continues to be something that is is visible and known by the developers throughout the process um our recommendation this time is that the unified development code does not need changes in response to the direction provided by the resolution 6489 however we do understand that there still is a lot of discussion um and some concern from the community regarding just the requirement that they plant a specific tree and there isn't freedom to do whatever they would like when it comes to planting that discussion uh we believe would most be most appropriately handled through the unified development code annual review where comments are um

